Good point. However, the DECcassette actually requires the opposite. It 
requires a transparent leader and trailer on the tape, to detect the end 
of the tape.

Most tape systems I've used was rather manual, so you had to wind 
forward or back on your own by pressing a button, before starting the 
computer operations, so leader or not was totally irrelevant.

As for the DECcassette, it apparently also do direct drive, and do not 
have a capstan or rollers. As such, they (in manuals) specify that the 
tape of DECcassette is stronger than normal audio tape, and normal audio 
tape should not be used because it might break. (Who knows, maybe they 
just wanted to sell very expensive tapes...)

> Also, many cassette based phone answering systems needed
> leaderless tapes, though the GE one I used to have knew how
> to skip the leader.

Seems like the only sensible thing to do.

	Johnny


