On 03/13/12 00:42, Roger Ivie wrote:
> On 2012-03-12, Johnny Billquist<bqt@softjar.se>  wrote:
>> Good point. However, the DECcassette actually requires the opposite. It
>> requires a transparent leader and trailer on the tape, to detect the end
>> of the tape.
>
> A similar Burroughs cassette system that I used on a B800 also
> required transparent leaders, for the same reason.

So did Wang and others. I modded a Wang dual cassette drive to work with an
aim65 board around 1984 ish. It was much faster than the original and 
included
a search facility to the required block, but was fairly primitive in 
comparison
to a proper file system with directory structure etc. An Apple II with 5 
meg hard
drive arrived which made the cassette system obsolete overnight :-).

I think there is an international standard covering Philips type compact
cassettes for data use. The ones I remember did have a transparent 
leader, but
also a small hole in the tape a few inches before the leader for opto 
sensing
end of tape. The digital drives were a work of art in comparison with 
the audio
version. The Wang drives were dual capstan 3 motor solenoid operated 
designs.
Ball bearing spindles and very smooth in operation...

Regards,

Chris



